WEBVTT

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:29.440
Surely one of the most remarkable animals that have ever existed and certainly one of the most famous is a dinosaur: Tyrannosaurus rex.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:35.810
an animal to spark the imagination for all of us.

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:38.320
What kind of an animal was it?

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.230
What did it look like?

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.760
How did it live?

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.740
Now, scientific research has answered such questions,

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.080
and not just about T-Rex,

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.640
but the other species that lived alongside it.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.690
And the latest imaging technology

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.450
enables us to bring them all to life.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.430
Planet Earth, 66 million years ago.

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:17.180
Skies are filled with flying giants.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.940
In the seas, monstrous reptiles patrol the depths.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:29.030
On land, dinosaurs of every kind.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.780
All facing the struggle to survive.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:45.390
We now know so much about the world that was ruled by the dinosaurs.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:52.500
This is their story.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:26.780
The southern shores of the Great Inland Sea that splits North America and the tracks of the land's top predator.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:36.500
A Tyrannosaurus Rex taking a swim.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:52.600
Hollow air-filled bones and powerful hind limbs make T-Rex very effective swimmers.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:58.580
This is an adult male with his young family.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:06.850
His offspring are now just old enough to follow him on a journey

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:10.980
to one of the many offshore islands that fringe this coast.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.770
It's a short enough crossing, but it can still be a dangerous one.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:52.690
Mosasaur, a giant marine lizard, over twice the size of a tyrannosaur and weighing over 15 tons.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.800
It's the largest predator on the planet.

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:17.060
Pettles are normally on a Mosasaur's menu.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:23.360
This brood would make a welcome snack.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:44.430
An adult T-Rex, even in water, could still defend itself very effectively, but the young

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:45.750
must stay close.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:37.210
Tyrannosaurus rex often lose at least two-thirds of their original brood of 15 or so in the

00:05:37.210 --> 00:06:00.000
first year. Now there are only four. The island offers safety and the problem

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:11.660
of food. Giant two-ton turtles are here to nest. But this is the one that has

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:21.120
drawn him here. It's dead, and the Tyrannosaur has smelt its rotting body.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:35.470
If he can just get to the underbelly,

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:54.460
he will have over 2,000 pounds of easy meat,

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:58.900
the most powerful jaws in nature.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:06.630
and can bite with the force of over five tons.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:12.880
The young are keen for a taste, but he's not sharing it.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:18.260
It's time for them to learn to hunt for themselves.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:30.000
The beach, however, doesn't look very promising, but as the evening approaches,

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:31.720
start to change.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:40.150
Baby turtles are beginning to hatch

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:42.260
from the sand and rush

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:55.880
down to the sea. The young

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.040
T-Rex have been brought to an ideal

00:07:58.040 --> 00:07:59.440
training ground.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:10.120
Catchling turtles are the perfect size for a novice to tackle.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:23.200
It's for any hunter to be inquisitive.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:41.980
There's a lot to learn.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:09:00.000
There's enough food here to satisfy both parent and young.

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:15.010
And if all else fails, you can always steal supper from someone else.

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:42.950
Across the globe, these shallow coastal seas cover an area of 25 million square miles,

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:45.810
far more than even the largest continent.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:53.650
That, combined with the richness of the waters, makes them very important habitat.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:06.390
Wherever land meets sea, nutrients rise from the deep, fueling an abundance of life.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:16.290
It's especially rich here in the North Atlantic, where huge shoals of fish come close to the shore.

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.610
One kind of animal thrives in such places.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:40.100
and forms immense colonies: flying reptiles, pterosaurs.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:46.200
Here on the beaches of North Africa, there are seven different species of them.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:53.410
They come here to feed, to rest, and to raise their young.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:11:00.000
Tethydraco are well adapted to spend time on the ground.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.660
and not only make their nests here,

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:08.500
but stay to protect their brood.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:13.180
And their young certainly need protection.

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:20.530
The dagger beak of Phosphatodraco.

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:30.000
A nine-foot-tall predator

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:41.970
that stalks through these colonies looking for the chance to snatch an unguarded hatchling.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:46.110
But some types of pterosaurs are less well adapted to life on land.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:52.500
They have a slightly different nesting strategy.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:58.740
They make their nests where they will attract less attention from predators.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.000
Isolated cliffs like this are.

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:09.760
Pterosaur eggs are leathery and can easily dry out, so they need to be covered.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:15.790
Beneath this pile of seaweed, something is stirring.

00:12:15.790 --> 00:12:28.130
A tiny Alcyone hatchling, just a few inches high and weighing less than two ounces.

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:37.430
Their mothers left the eggs here about two months ago.

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:54.720
Falling to each other synchronizes hatching.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.810
There is safety in numbers.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:05.350
Their first instinct is to climb.

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:44.500
from hundreds of nests gather on the clifftop to prepare for their first flight, but their

00:13:44.500 --> 00:13:51.070
wings are still unformed. The bones of the long finger that supports their wing membrane

00:13:51.070 --> 00:14:00.000
must first straighten and lock together, and that will take some hours. But they can't

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:14.400
for long. Their bones are extremely light, up to 90% air, and that makes the effort needed to take

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:25.040
off much easier. Even so, test flights are essential. There will only be one chance to

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:27.640
get it right when the time comes to launch.

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:42.280
The cliff edge creates updrafts and they can help, so it pays to gather there facing into

00:14:42.280 --> 00:15:00.000
the prevailing winds and towards the mainland, but no one seems quite ready to take the plunge.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:05.760
At last, one youngster sets them all on their way.

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:47.650
They're not heading for the beach and its colony.

00:15:47.650 --> 00:15:51.890
They need to get to the mist forest that lies beyond.

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:09.040
Barbarodactylus, powerful predatory pterosaurs that normally catch fish, but the hatchlings

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:20.080
are too good to miss.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:25.190
One way to take evasive action is to simply fold their wings and drop.

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:36.020
But losing height will make it harder for them to reach the forests.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:18.180
Crash landing in the colony, it's no place for the hatchling on its own.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:51.510
lucky survivor from the first wave of hatchlings, still heading in the right direction.

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:28.100
Of the hatchlings that left the stack, few get as far as this, but for them, this forest offers all the shelter and food that a young pterosaur needs.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:35.680
For the next five years, this will be their home.

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:48.780
Then they will be large enough to join the adults catching fish out on the open ocean.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:57.210
Animals that spend all their lives fishing out at sea,

00:18:57.210 --> 00:19:00.000
must occasionally visit the coast for over time.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:08.000
particular purpose. In the waters of the drowned continent of Zeelandia, a long

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:15.020
journey is coming to an end. These are Tarangisaurs, a type of huge marine

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:28.370
reptile nearly 30 feet long. This female is accompanied by her calf, about six

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:29.480
months old.

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:37.780
At most, she'll have only one youngster every two years or so.

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:43.820
It's a huge investment, and one that makes the bond between mother and young very important.

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:59.770
She has brought her young calf many miles to this one bay.

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:13.850
And they're not alone.

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:17.860
Turangisaurs come here from across the South Pacific.

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:50.370
Males also gather here to display to females, but for now courtship is not the female's

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:56.710
first priority.

00:20:56.710 --> 00:21:00.000
This bay has something that few others can provide.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:24.850
pebbles that are particularly smooth, hard, and rounded.

00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:27.650
They've been worn by the action of river water,

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:29.350
but they're hard to find.

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:43.950
Here, however, in the pool at the bottom of the waterfall, there are plenty of them, and the Turangisaws can take their pick.

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:51.990
They then do something rather remarkable.

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:53.960
They swallow them.

00:21:56.250 --> 00:22:00.000
They need the stones to act both as ballast and as...

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:07.280
gizzard stones, gastroliths, which will remain in their stomachs to grind up their untued

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:22.480
food. For a youngster, learning to swallow pebbles for the first time isn't easy. It

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:30.000
takes a little practice. But it's also a chance for his mother to find

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.760
a suitable mate amongst the males.

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:17.010
For the calf, at last, success.

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:24.850
He will swallow as many as he can.

00:23:25.240 --> 00:23:29.100
And as he grows, he'll return here for more.

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:38.890
It's time for the family to leave the coast

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:41.270
and head back out to sea to feed.

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:43.760
And for the young to orangisaur,

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:47.340
that is an important step on the road to adulthood.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:59.760
In Southern Europe,

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.740
where the Atlantic meets the great Tethys Sea,

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:09.110
Coast of Life reaches perhaps its greatest diversity.

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:12.270
Rising sea level means that there are countless

00:24:12.270 --> 00:24:27.990
submerged islands covered with sponges, clams and corals.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:30.000
Take advantage of the sunshine

00:24:30.280 --> 00:24:44.120
shallows, forming partnerships with algae that grow within their tissues.

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:48.630
They collect tiny particles of food floating in the ocean currents.

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:09.410
Myriads of these marine creatures encross the solid rock.

00:25:10.410 --> 00:25:18.180
But one rock here appears surprisingly bare.

00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:25.250
Beyond is the sheer drop-off and the deep sea,

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:28.230
home to oceanic predators.

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:47.380
and danger for unwary reef fish.

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:20.350
But this pycnodont fish has little to fear.

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:25.160
This is Hoffman's Mosasaur, the ocean's deadliest predator.

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:27.700
But he's not here to eat.

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:29.640
He's come to be cleaned.

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:40.480
Mosasaurs are giant lizards and have both a lizard's forked tongue

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:44.630
and, during the mating season, a lizard's colorful skin.

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:50.360
Now it's time to shed old skin.

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:07.940
And when you need to look your best, nothing but an all-over body scrub will do.

00:27:07.940 --> 00:27:23.430
Shrimp, pick scales shed from his body.

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:49.650
Resting at the surface allows this sea-going, air-breathing lizard to fill his lungs, relax.

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:11.840
A rival, a younger male challenging for this territory.

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:40.690
The old male is heavier, over 15 tons.

00:28:40.690 --> 00:29:00.000
But his rival is more nimble, as evenly matched as this.

00:29:00.090 --> 00:29:07.330
can be lethal. Mosasaurs have been found with the shattered teeth of rivals

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:28.960
embedded in their skulls. The male snatches the breath, the advantage of

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:37.300
By dragging his rival down, he could drown him.

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:28.220
has triumphed for now

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:29.920
at least this reef

00:30:30.120 --> 00:30:50.480
is still his territory crescent moon off the coast of north america the calm

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:56.120
dark nights that follow trigger a rare and beautiful event

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:08.540
Tonight, even in the deep, there is light.

00:31:34.990 --> 00:31:38.830
Ammonites are mollusks related to octopus and squid.

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:44.020
These scophytids are no bigger than a human hand.

00:31:44.020 --> 00:31:55.760
For weeks, they've been gathering in the coastal depths.

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:07.360
In the deep, these lights may help lure Plankton as food.

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:11.750
But tonight, they serve a different purpose.

00:32:11.750 --> 00:32:17.200
They've been drawn to the surface to mate.

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:20.740
And soon, there are thousands in each shoal.

00:32:34.510 --> 00:32:40.640
Neighbor triggers neighbor, creating waves of bioluminescence.

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:55.700
Complex nervous systems controlling light-producing cells called photocytes.

00:33:04.250 --> 00:33:09.790
The males jostle for a place around the larger females.

00:33:09.790 --> 00:33:17.370
Clues from their displays tell her who is the fittest and who is the best mate.

00:33:17.370 --> 00:33:25.860
As couples entwine, they coordinate their light displays.

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:36.950
If he can't match her precise rhythm, he'll be rejected.

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:44.340
But there is perfect synchrony.

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:51.830
This pair will now spawn and help produce the next generation.

00:33:51.830 --> 00:34:00.000
After fertilization, the females end

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:14.720
the shallows to release their eggs. As with most cephalopods, breeding is the

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:27.360
final act of their short lives. By morning, these lights will have flickered and died.

00:34:37.450 --> 00:34:41.430
This magical night will be their last.

00:34:49.310 --> 00:35:00.000
All over the globe, the coast provides an ideal place for countless

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:06.260
animals to mate, raise their young, and to feed.

00:35:06.260 --> 00:35:19.110
Back in the shallow waters of Zeelandia, large groups of twerangosaurs have come together.

00:35:34.060 --> 00:35:37.140
They propel themselves with all four fins

00:35:37.220 --> 00:35:41.100
and travel almost effortlessly to find the fish shoals

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:42.930
that gather here in summer.

00:35:43.540 --> 00:35:48.020
Occasionally, they break the surface to gulp air

00:35:48.100 --> 00:35:51.250
before continuing on their underwater flight.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:04.510
But one female is not swimming with her usual grace.

00:36:04.510 --> 00:36:10.720
She and her two-year-old calf are lagging behind the rest of the group.

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:23.520
She's moving rather laboriously, and that has not gone unnoticed.

00:36:23.520 --> 00:36:29.960
A deadly hunter, Kai Kai Filu.

00:36:36.080 --> 00:36:39.540
The apparently stricken female is a tempting target.

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:47.240
Diving to deeper water might make her less vulnerable.

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:57.620
For the calf, trying to distract the Mosasaur is a dangerous game.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:22.940
it's buying time. The mother and calf are not entirely alone. Individuals may be

00:37:22.940 --> 00:37:30.000
related and it's in all their interests to drive Kaikaifidu away.

00:37:53.420 --> 00:38:00.000
This is the reason for the female's apparent distress: She's pregnant,

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:30.000
And now, after a one-and-a-half-year pregnancy, the baby...

00:38:36.910 --> 00:38:41.740
over 10 feet long, nearly half the length of its mother.

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:52.880
One of the biggest babies of all time.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:58.680
It needs to get to the surface to take its very first breath.

00:39:19.900 --> 00:39:23.930
This young to a Rangisaur could live for 80 years.

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:30.000
Now, supported by her family, she can take her place.

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:44.050
As a predator, in one of the richest habitats on Earth, the sea is around the coasts of our prehistoric planet.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:07.220
On prehistoric planet, giant dinosaurs wrestle to win a mate in baiting-hot deserts.

00:40:07.600 --> 00:40:13.580
And across the globe, remarkable specialists must use every trick they can

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:17.840
to survive in the most inhospitable places on Earth.

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:26.050
To discover the science behind the stories, go now to the prehistoric planet show page.
